---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import TileCalculator from '../../components/TileCalculator.astro';
import { webApplicationSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../utils/schema';
import { CALCULATORS_HUB } from '../../data/tools';

const path = '/calculators/tile-calculator';
const title = 'Tile Calculator: Square Feet, Tiles & Boxes';
const description =
  'Estimate how many tiles and boxes you need for a rectangular or known-area project. Adjust tile size, layout waste, and package information.';

const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: CALCULATORS_HUB.title, href: '/calculators' },
  { label: 'Tile Calculator' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  schema={[
    webApplicationSchema({
      name: 'Tile Calculator',
      description,
      path,
    }),
    breadcrumbSchema(crumbs),
  ]}
>
  <div class="container-site container-calc calc-page">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />

    <header class="calc-page__intro">
      <p class="category-eyebrow calc-page__eyebrow"><a href="/calculators" class="text-brand no-underline hover:underline">Calculators</a></p>
      <h1 class="section-heading">Tile Calculator</h1>
      <p class="section-lede calc-page__lede">
        Estimate how many tiles — and optional boxes — you need for a rectangular or known-area project. Adjust tile
        size and layout waste to match your plan.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="calc-page__tool">
      <TileCalculator />
    </div>

    <p class="calc-page__disclaimer">
      This calculator estimates tile quantity from project area, tile size, and the waste allowance you choose. It does
      not model the exact layout, edge cuts, offcut reuse, substrate conditions, waterproofing, mortar, grout, movement
      joints, structural suitability, or code requirements. Check the tile carton or product listing for package
      coverage and confirm installation requirements with the manufacturer.
    </p>

    <article class="prose-article calc-page__copy">
      <h2>How the tile calculation works</h2>
      <p>
        The calculator converts your project to square feet, then divides by the face area of one tile. A waste
        percentage increases that quantity for cuts, breakage, and leftover pieces, then the result is rounded up to
        whole tiles. Optional packaging converts the purchase count into boxes using either tiles per box or coverage
        per box from the product you plan to buy. Grout gap is not included in the quantity math.
      </p>

      <h2>How much extra tile should you buy?</h2>
      <p>
        Several manufacturers and suppliers commonly suggest about 10% extra for straightforward layouts and a larger
        allowance — often around 15% — for diagonal or more intricate patterns. Projects with many corners, jogs, or
        complex patterns may need still more. These are planning allowances, not universal requirements. The
        percentage in this tool is editable so you can follow your product guidance or installer recommendation. Keeping
        leftover tile from the same dye lot also helps with future repairs.
      </p>

      <h2>Straight vs diagonal tile layouts</h2>
      <p>
        A straight (grid) layout typically produces fewer angled cuts at the walls. A diagonal layout turns the grid,
        which often creates more triangular offcuts that are harder to reuse. That is why this calculator suggests a
        higher waste percentage for diagonal layouts. It does not provide installation instructions or a full layout
        plan — dry-laying and professional layout advice still matter for the finished look.
      </p>

      <h2>How many tiles are in a box?</h2>
      <p>
        There is no generic tiles-per-box count. Packaging varies by product, size, and brand. Enter the tiles-per-box
        or square-feet-per-box value printed on the carton or product listing. When you enable box calculation, choose
        one method; the calculator does not invent a default package size.
      </p>

      <h2>Tile size and square footage</h2>
      <p>
        Face area is width × length. Useful examples before waste:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>12 × 12 in ≈ 1 sq ft per tile</li>
        <li>12 × 24 in ≈ 2 sq ft per tile</li>
        <li>24 × 48 in ≈ 8 sq ft per tile</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Dividing project area by face area gives a theoretical count. Real installations still need waste for cuts and
        breakage, especially with large-format tile.
      </p>

      <h2>Example calculations</h2>
      <h3>10 × 10 ft area, 12 × 12 in tile, 10% waste</h3>
      <p>
        Project area = 100 sq ft. Face area = 1 sq ft. Raw count = 100 tiles. With 10% waste → purchase 110 tiles. At 10
        tiles per box → 11 boxes.
      </p>
      <h3>10 × 10 ft area, 12 × 24 in tile, 10% waste</h3>
      <p>
        Face area = 2 sq ft. Raw count = 50 tiles. With 10% waste → purchase 55 tiles. At 16 tiles per box → 4 boxes.
      </p>
      <h3>Known 120 sq ft, 12 × 24 in tile, 10% waste</h3>
      <p>
        Raw count = 60 tiles. With 10% waste → purchase 66 tiles.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>How much tile do I need?</h3>
      <p>
        Divide the project area by one tile’s face area, add your waste allowance, and round up to whole tiles. Use the
        calculator for unit conversion and packaging in one step.
      </p>
      <h3>How much extra tile should I buy?</h3>
      <p>
        About 10% is a common starting point for straight layouts; about 15% is often suggested for diagonal work.
        Confirm with your product guidance or installer — no percentage fits every job.
      </p>
      <h3>How many 12×24 tiles cover 100 sq ft?</h3>
      <p>
        Each 12 × 24 in tile has about 2 sq ft of face area, so 100 ÷ 2 = 50 tiles before waste. With 10% waste, plan on
        55 tiles.
      </p>
      <h3>How many 12×12 tiles are in a square foot?</h3>
      <p>
        One 12 × 12 in tile is about 1 sq ft of face area, so one tile covers about one square foot before joints and
        waste.
      </p>
      <h3>How do I calculate boxes of tile?</h3>
      <p>
        Divide the purchase tile count by tiles per box, or divide the waste-adjusted area by coverage per box, then
        round up. Use the value from your product listing.
      </p>
      <h3>Does diagonal tile use more material?</h3>
      <p>
        Often yes, because angled perimeter cuts create more unused offcuts. That is why diagonal layouts usually use a
        higher waste allowance.
      </p>
      <h3>Should I keep extra tile?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes when practical. Matching dye lots later can be difficult if a product is discontinued or batch colors shift.
        Store leftovers labeled with the lot information from the carton.
      </p>
      <h3>Do grout lines change how much tile I need?</h3>
      <p>
        Grout joints can affect the exact physical layout and may slightly change how many tiles fit a space. This
        calculator estimates quantity from tile face area and uses the waste allowance to account for real-world cuts,
        breakage, and layout inefficiency. It does not calculate grout quantity.
      </p>
      <h3>Can I use this for backsplash or wall tile?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes for quantity. Enter the flat area you plan to cover. The tool does not choose adhesives, waterproofing, or
        installation methods.
      </p>
      <h3>Can I use this for shower tile?</h3>
      <p>
        You can estimate flat surface area for shopping, but this calculator does not determine waterproofing,
        substrate, slope, installation system, or code requirements for wet areas.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.cletile.com/blogs/cle-education/how-much-extra-tile-to-buy">Clé Tile — how much extra tile to buy</a>
          — planning discussion of roughly 10% for standard layouts and about 15% for intricate or diagonal work
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.bedrosians.com/en/blog/articles/how-to-plan-a-tile-layout-before-you-set-a-single-tile/">Bedrosians — how to plan a tile layout</a>
          — layout planning notes and approximate overage by pattern (about 10% straight/offset; about 15% diagonal)
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://merolatile.com/frequently-asked-questions/">Merola Tile FAQ</a>
          — measuring square footage and waste guidance for standard installs versus rooms with many cuts
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.daltile.com/how-to/faqs">Daltile FAQ</a>
          — attic stock / ordering extra tile for dye-lot matching and future repairs
        </li>
        <li>
          American Olean / retail product listings — commonly recommend about 10% overage for patterns, angles, and cuts
          (verify on the specific product page)
        </li>
        <li>
          BuildDirect tile &amp; stone installation guidance — order roughly 10–15% more; diagonal and patterned layouts
          may need more; keep leftovers for repairs
        </li>
        <li>
          L’Antic Colonial ceramic tile installation guide — recommends ordering about 10–15% more than calculated
          material for cuts and future repairs
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
